{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "control": "S"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 139, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Removing a redundant conv on the input-output edge costs little, while removing a skip there (the alternative parents) destroys the residual path and hurts."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a residual connection where none existed improves trainability of the stacked network more than swapping it in for an existing op."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing the weakest op (parameter-free pooling) on the node2-to-output edge with a 3x3 conv gives a larger gain than replacing a skip or another conv."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 0 is none, node 1 is dead, so adding a conv reactivates the paths through edges 2 and 4 and adds depth and capacity."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Deleting a conv on the edge into the output removes real learned capacity, whereas deleting a skip or pool there is often neutral or helpful."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Removing a conv on edge 1 is mild because node 2 still gets conv input via node 1, but removing a skip on edge 1 cuts a short gradient path and hurts more."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Swapping a 3x3 conv for pooling loses capacity, whereas swapping none or skip for pooling at worst adds a cheap smoothing path."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "w